+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches rather than ad-hoc code. JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) is already in place as the building block for addressing values; JSON Patch is the next piece.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add `applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, implementing JSON Patch (RFC 6902) with its six operations: `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`.
+- The function returns the patched document as a new value and never mutates `document` or `patch`.
+- It throws a new exported `JsonPatchError` when the patch is invalid (RFC 6902 §3, §4) or an operation fails (including a failed `test`, §4.6). A failing patch has no partial effect (RFC 6902 §5).
+- Not breaking: purely additive; existing exports are unchanged.
+
+**Governing standards:** RFC 6902 (§3 document structure, §4.1–4.6 operations, §5 error handling), using JSON Pointer RFC 6901 for `path`/`from`.
+
+**Out of scope:** JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396), patch generation/diffing, in-place application, returning partial results, and the `application/json-patch+json` media type / HTTP handling.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: Applying an RFC 6902 JSON Patch to a JSON document, non-mutating, with validation and error behaviour.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+<!-- None: json-pointer requirements are unchanged. -->
+
+## Impact
+
+- `src/index.ts`: new `applyPatch` and `JsonPatchError` exports, plus internal pointer parsing reuse.
+- `test/patch.test.ts`: new tests.
+- No new dependencies; no `package.json` change.
